    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-22T11:09:38.037Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: restore refcount ordering for lockless socket access\n\nBefore commit fb041bb7c0a9 (\"locking/refcount: Consolidate implementations\nof refcount_t\"), default builds implemented refcount_inc_not_zero() using\natomic_add_unless(), which provided full memory ordering on success. The\ncommit removed that ordering on weakly ordered architectures, allowing\naccesses after a successful reference acquisition to be reordered before\nit.\n\nUse refcount_inc_not_zero_acquire() in the IPv4 and IPv6 established\nlookups and in inet_twsk_purge(), where the socket is validated again\nafter taking a reference.\n\nbpf_sk_assign() can receive a socket pointer obtained through BTF\nwithout a reference. Use acquire ordering when it takes its own reference\nfor a refcounted socket.\n\nUse refcount_set_release() when setting refcounts in the SYN cookie,\nTCP Fast Open, request migration, and TIME_WAIT paths. Keep the existing\nTIME_WAIT smp_wmb(), which separately orders the refcount store before\nehash publication."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 9.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "CRITICAL"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - A TCP segment enters __inet_lookup_established() from tcp_v4_rcv() through __inet_lookup_skb(), and from tcp_v4_early_demux() in ip_rcv_finish_core(); tcp_v6_rcv() does the same via __inet6_lookup_established(). The header 4-tuple is the lookup key, and that segment is delivered to whatever socket the unordered refcount_inc_not_zero() returns.\nAC:L - The attacker produces the race by opening and closing connections and by completing SYN-cookie handshakes, so tcp_get_cookie_sock() and connect/close recycle SLAB_TYPESAFE_BY_RCU sockets while further segments enter the lookup. Relaxed refcount_inc_not_zero() does not order the following inet_match() or hlist_nulls_unhashed_lockless() recheck, and no victim action or hardware fault is required.\nPR:N - tcp_v4_rcv() and tcp_v4_early_demux() run on every incoming TCP segment before TCP-MD5, TCP-AO, or application authentication. Sending the segment to a listening port performs no credential check on this path.\nUI:N - The attacker both recycles the sockets and sends the racing segments. No local user must mount media, open a file, or accept a connection.\nS:U - The lookup, the recycled tcp_sock or request_sock, and any dst_entry reached from sk_rx_dst all belong to the host kernel. The bug does not cross a guest-to-host or IOMMU boundary.\nC:H - A load of sk_rx_dst after the relaxed refcount_inc_not_zero() can still observe the previous tcp_sock's route, which tcp_disconnect() already passed to dst_release(). tcp_v4_early_demux() and tcp_v4_do_rcv() then read dst->obsolete and call dst->ops->check on that freed dst_entry.\nI:H - tcp_v4_do_rcv() dispatches through the stale sk_rx_dst via dst->ops->check and can dst_release() it, writing a freed dst_entry. It also passes the attacker's segment to tcp_rcv_established() on the recycled socket, updating that socket's sequence state and receive queue.\nA:H - If reuse returns the unhashed SYN-cookie request published by tcp_get_cookie_sock(), its rsk_listener is NULL and tcp_v4_rcv() reads sk->sk_state at offset 0x12 through that NULL pointer in softirq, oopsing the host. The freed dst_entry dereference faults as well."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
