    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:05:53.633Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nxfs: cap the block count of xfs_iomap_write_direct() at one extent\n\nxfs_iomap_write_direct() reserves blocks for the whole count in an\nunsigned int. For a pNFS layout the client chooses that count, and an\nRW LAYOUTGET for 16 TiB wraps the reservation and shuts XFS down.\n\nxfs_bmapi_write() maps at most XFS_MAX_BMBT_EXTLEN blocks here, so cap\nthe count at that. nfsd asks again for the rest. The other callers get\nthe same mapping as before."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.5,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - The attacker-controlled value is loga_length in an NFSv4.1 OP_LAYOUTGET (nfsd4_decode_layoutget). nfsd4_block_proc_layoutget passes it to xfs_fs_map_blocks, which calls xfs_iomap_write_direct with that block count; NFS over TCP is a routable transport.\nAC:L - The client creates a non-reflink, non-realtime file so xfs_fs_map_blocks sees a hole, and chooses loga_length so the block count truncates when XFS_DIOSTRAT_SPACE_RES is stored in unsigned int dblocks (2^32 blocks is 16TiB at 4K). xfs_bmapi_write then allocates one extent up to XFS_MAX_BMBT_EXTLEN and xfs_trans_mod_sb finds t_blk_res_used greater than t_blk_res.\nPR:N - IOMODE_RW makes nfsd4_layoutget call fh_verify with NFSD_MAY_WRITE, and nfsd_setuser copies the RPC cr_uid into fsuid. On an AUTH_SYS export that uid is client-asserted; root_squash remaps only uid 0, so the attacker needs no authenticated account or capability.\nUI:N - The same client creates the file and sends the LAYOUTGET whose loga_length is the value that wraps the reservation.\nS:U - xfs_force_shutdown marks the XFS mount that served the LAYOUTGET, which is the same kernel security authority that holds the filesystem and nfsd.\nC:N - xfs_trans_commit fails and xfs_fs_map_blocks returns before xfs_bmbt_to_iomap, so OP_LAYOUTGET completes with an NFS error and no extent data. The fault is the t_blk_res accounting check in xfs_trans_mod_sb.\nI:N - After t_blk_res_used exceeds t_blk_res, xfs_force_shutdown stops the log, and __xfs_trans_commit returns -EIO before xlog_cil_commit, leaving the over-allocation out of the on-disk log.\nA:H - The wrapped dblocks value makes xfs_trans_mod_sb call xfs_force_shutdown(SHUTDOWN_CORRUPT_INCORE) from inside xfs_bmapi_write. That mount then fails further I/O with EIO until an administrator unmounts and remounts it."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
