{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99758",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.433Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:51:26.771Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:07:05.445Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:07:05.445Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nsctp: account for chunk padding in sctp_init_cause()\n\nsctp_init_cause() accepts a chunk when skb_tailroom() can hold the\ncause header and the payload. sctp_addto_chunk() then writes 0-3\nbytes of padding before that header so the chunk stays 4-byte\naligned. The check does not include the padding, and the following\nskb_put() calls skb_over_panic() from softirq.\n\nCount the padding sctp_addto_chunk() is about to write. The cause\nlength on the wire stays the header plus the payload.\n\nFound by LLM inspection, triggered and tested the fix."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:N/I:N/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.5,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:N - A remote peer's SCTP INIT (chunk type 1, verification tag 0) carries unrecognized parameters that sctp_sf_do_5_1B_init passes to sctp_verify_init and sctp_process_unk_param; those parameter lengths are what sctp_init_cause fails to pad. SCTP is IP protocol 132, so the packet is routable.\nAC:L - The attacker sets the parameter type's top bits to SKIP_ERR (0xC000) and a length not divisible by 4, so a later sctp_init_cause on that ERROR chunk sees padlen = SCTP_PAD4(chunklen)-chunklen while skb_tailroom still passes the pre-fix check. sctp_make_op_error_limited's size is deterministic and every length is chosen by the attacker.\nPR:N - sctp_sf_do_5_1B_init accepts this INIT with verification tag 0 in SCTP_STATE_CLOSED, before any association or AUTH shared key exists, and sctp_verify_init checks no credentials.\nUI:N - The attacker alone sends the INIT, and sctp_rcv delivers it through sctp_inq_push in softirq.\nS:U - skb_over_panic in sctp_addto_chunk stays in the host kernel SCTP input path that parsed the INIT; the affected authority is that same host.\nC:N - skb_put() in sctp_addto_chunk() calls skb_over_panic() and BUG() before skb_put_data() copies the cause, so the shortfall ends at the oops.\nI:N - The uncounted pad is 1-3 bytes, and skb_put() BUGs when skb->tail passes skb->end, before memcpy of the INIT parameter.\nA:H - The failed skb_put() calls skb_over_panic() from sctp_init_cause during sctp_inq_push() in softirq, and oops_end() panics with \"Fatal exception in interrupt\" because in_interrupt() is set."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
