    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99744",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.432Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:51:16.049Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:52.051Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:52.051Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet: atl1c: drop oversized RX frames instead of panicking\n\natl1c sizes its receive buffers from the netdev MTU plus a fixed set of L2\noverheads, which comes to 1522 bytes at the default MTU.  A link partner\nthat sends a longer frame - in practice a MikroTik CCR2004-1G-2XS-PCIe,\nwhose RouterOS side defaults to l2mtu 1600 while the host stays at MTU 1500\n- makes the length reported by the RX return descriptor exceed what the\nhardware wrote, and skb_put() then either includes bytes that were never\nreceived or trips skb_over_panic and takes the host down.\n\nCompare the descriptor length against buffer_info->length, the length the\nhardware was given, rather than against skb_tailroom(): the skb carries 64\nbytes of slack for the DMA erratum, so tailroom is larger than the DMA\nbuffer and a frame in between would pass the check and still be delivered\nwith a tail the device never wrote.  The same test rejects frames shorter\nthan ETH_HLEN + ETH_FCS_LEN; before this change a reported length below\nETH_FCS_LEN underflowed in skb_put().\n\nThe frame is dropped with kfree_skb_reason(SKB_DROP_REASON_PKT_TOO_BIG) so\ndrop_monitor and the kfree_skb tracepoint see it, and counted through\ndev_core_stats_rx_dropped_inc(), which survives atl1c_get_stats()\noverwriting the hardware counters on every read.  It also counts as work:\natl1c_clean_rfd() has already recycled the RFD, and the refill at the end\nof the poll only runs when work_done is non-zero, so sustained oversized\ntraffic would otherwise drain the ring and stop RX.\n\nTested on a MikroTik CCR2004-1G-2XS-PCIe (4x AR8151) in a Proxmox VE host,\nwith atl1c built from a DKMS tree carrying this change: the l2mtu-1600\ntraffic that panics the stock driver is dropped and counted with the patch\napplied, and received normally once the host MTU is raised to match.\n\nThis complements commit 36c2009d90f2 (\"net: atl1c: fix soft lockup on\nout-of-range tpd_cons read\"), which fixed the TX side of the same\n\"host dies while the card resets\" problem."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:A/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:L/I:N/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.1,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:A - The attacker-controlled value is RRS_PKT_SIZE in the RX return descriptor (rrs->word3), written by the MAC from the Ethernet frame a link partner sent. atl1c_intr() schedules NAPI and atl1c_clean_rx() feeds that length to skb_put(). That length is the on-wire frame size, and only a station on this link can present a frame longer than the 1522-byte RX buffer.\nAC:L - atl1c_set_rxbufsize() keeps rx_buffer_len at AT_RX_BUF_SIZE (1522) for the default MTU, and atl1c_configure_mac() programs REG_MTU and REG_RX_BUF_SIZE from it. The attacker sends a longer frame; the tested l2mtu-1600 traffic reaches skb_put() because atl1c_clean_rx() drops a descriptor only when RRS_RX_ERR_SUM or RRS_802_3_LEN_ERR is set, and this frame sets neither.\nPR:N - The frame is accepted under the station address from atl1c_hw_set_mac_addr() or as broadcast because atl1c_start_mac() sets MAC_CTRL_BC_EN. atl1c_intr(), atl1c_intr_rx_tx(), and atl1c_clean_rx() apply no credential check before skb_put() of RRS_PKT_SIZE.\nUI:N - The attacker's frame alone raises the NIC IRQ into atl1c_intr(), which schedules the NAPI poll atl1c_clean_rx(), while the host MTU stays at its default.\nS:U - The panic and the stale skb tail stay in the host kernel that owns the atl1c NIC, the same security authority that received the frame.\nC:L - When RRS_PKT_SIZE is above buffer_info->length (the dma_map_single() length from atl1c_alloc_rx_buffer()) but skb_put() still fits the rx_buffer_len+64 skb, atl1c_clean_rx() includes bytes the MAC never wrote. ip_rcv_core() keeps that tail when tot_len covers it, and ip_forward() sends it onward. The exposed region is the 64-byte DMA-erratum slack.\nI:N - skb_put() only advances skb->tail and skb->len. Device DMA stays inside the buffer_info->length mapping from atl1c_alloc_rx_buffer(), and a length past skb->end stops in skb_over_panic().\nA:H - A descriptor length that makes skb_put() exceed the skb, which the tested link-partner l2mtu of 1600 does, calls skb_over_panic() from atl1c_clean_rx() in the NAPI poll scheduled by atl1c_intr(). BUG() in that softirq brings the host down."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
