{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99690",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.429Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:50:34.973Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:04.740Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:04.740Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nocfs2: cache the mount context for dentry lock callbacks\n\nocfs2_drop_dentry_lock() drops the inode reference before releasing the\nDENTRY lock.  This lets inode eviction release the local inode and open\nlocks before a remote unlink can proceed past the DENTRY lock.\n\nA DLM callback can still arrive before the DENTRY lock has been dropped.\nocfs2_get_dentry_osb() obtains the mount context through dl_inode->i_sb,\nwhich can access a freed inode when the downconvert thread drops the\nlast inode reference in its post-unlock callback:\n\n  Downconvert thread                     DLM callback thread\n  ------------------                     -------------------\n  ocfs2_process_blocked_lock()\n    ocfs2_dentry_post_unlock()\n      ocfs2_dentry_lock_put()\n        ocfs2_drop_dentry_lock()\n          iput(dl->dl_inode)\n          [inode reclaimed after RCU]\n                                         ocfs2_blocking_ast()\n                                           ocfs2_get_dentry_osb()\n                                             dl->dl_inode->i_sb\n          ocfs2_simple_drop_lockres()\n\nStore the mount context in the DENTRY lock resource's l_priv field and use\nthe default mount-context lookup.  Recover the containing dentry lock with\ncontainer_of(), as already done for refcount locks, and remove\nocfs2_get_dentry_osb().  ocfs2_dismount_volume() shuts down the DLM before\nfreeing the mount context, so callbacks can use it without accessing the\ninode.  Preserve the existing iput-before-unlock order to avoid delaying\nlocal inode and open lock release until after the DENTRY lock is released."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - ocfs2_get_dentry_osb() reads dl->dl_inode->i_sb from ocfs2_blocking_ast() after this node attached the lock in ocfs2_lookup() via ocfs2_dentry_attach_lock(). A peer ocfs2_unlink() only reaches that callback through an ordinary EX request in ocfs2_remote_dentry_delete(); the inode iput() frees was created by that local lookup.\nAC:L - The attacker repeats a local ocfs2_lookup() and a peer unlink. The EX request makes ocfs2_dentry_convert_worker() return UNBLOCK_STOP_POST so ocfs2_drop_dentry_lock() iput()s the inode; the first BAST runs before that iput. dlm_flush_asts() clears highest_blocked, so dropping a second node's PR holder makes dlm_shuffle_lists() queue a later BAST into ocfs2_blocking_ast() after reclaim and before ocfs2_simple_drop_lockres() returns.\nPR:L - ocfs2_lookup() and ocfs2_unlink() perform no capability check, and ocfs2_dentry_lock() skips the cluster lock only for ocfs2_mount_local() or a hard-readonly volume. A local user who can look up the name and unlink it (directory write) on an already clustered mount reaches both sides of the race.\nUI:N - The attacker issues the ocfs2_lookup() that igrab()s dl_inode in ocfs2_dentry_attach_lock() and the unlink that starts the downconvert. No other user has to open the file or mount the volume.\nS:U - The ocfs2_super derived from the freed inode is the mount on the node that holds the dentry lock, and ocfs2_wake_downconvert_thread() only wakes that node's dc_task. The fault stays in the same kernel security authority.\nC:H - ocfs2_get_dentry_osb() loads i_sb and s_fs_info from the ocfs2_inode_info after ocfs2_drop_dentry_lock()'s iput() has queued it for RCU free through ocfs2_free_inode(). Reclaim of that ocfs2_inode_cachep object lets the callback consume freed memory as a pointer.\nI:H - ocfs2_wake_downconvert_thread() runs on every BAST after that load: it spin_lock_irqsave()s osb->dc_task_lock, increments osb->dc_wake_sequence, and wake_up()s osb->dc_event. Those stores follow i_sb and s_fs_info taken from the freed ocfs2_inode_info.\nA:H - If the reclaimed inode's i_sb is unmapped, poisoned, or not a super_block, the s_fs_info load in OCFS2_SB() faults inside ocfs2_blocking_ast(), before ocfs2_simple_drop_lockres() finishes dropping the DENTRY lock."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
