            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/mmu_notifier: set ->mm only after mmgrab()\n\nRadeon uses a non-NULL notifier->mm to decide whether to call\nmmu_interval_notifier_remove() when freeing a buffer object.\n__mmu_interval_notifier_insert() sets ->mm before validating the range,\nso a wrapping userptr range can leave ->mm set after insertion fails.\nCleanup then tries to remove an unregistered notifier for which no mm\nreference was acquired.\n\nSet ->mm only after mmgrab(), once insertion can no longer fail. While\nwe could fix radeon's code instead, setting ->mm on success only makes\nthe code more robust.\n\nFix generated by an LLM and sent by Chris Mason in private.\n\n[ david: simplify description ]"
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The wrapping range is the addr and size of DRM_IOCTL_RADEON_GEM_USERPTR, passed by radeon_gem_userptr_ioctl into radeon_mn_register then mmu_interval_notifier_insert/__mmu_interval_notifier_insert. Those bytes come from the local ioctl argument, not from a remote protocol or from disk.\nAC:L - radeon_gem_userptr_ioctl only rejects a non-page-aligned addr|size, so the caller can choose a range whose start+length-1 overflows check_add_overflow. The -EOVERFLOW path always drops the GEM object, and radeon_gem_object_free calls radeon_mn_unregister. The attacker issues that ioctl; no external state is required.\nPR:L - radeon_ioctls_kms marks RADEON_GEM_USERPTR DRM_AUTH|DRM_RENDER_ALLOW, and drm_ioctl_permit() skips DRM_AUTH for a render client. An unprivileged process that can open the radeon render node reaches radeon_gem_userptr_ioctl without CAP_SYS_ADMIN.\nUI:N - The attacker opens the radeon render node and calls radeon_gem_userptr_ioctl with the wrapping userptr. No other user has to mount media or act on the range.\nS:U - The unpaired mmdrop frees the caller's own mm_struct in the host kernel. That is kernel memory corruption in the same security authority as the radeon ioctl, the usual kernel privilege-escalation scope.\nC:H - On overflow __mmu_interval_notifier_insert set interval_sub->mm and returned before mmgrab, but radeon_mn_unregister still calls mmu_interval_notifier_remove, which mmdrops that mm. __mmdrop then pgd_free's and free_mm's a live mm (mm_init sets mm_count to 1), a page-table use-after-free that can disclose memory.\nI:H - The same unpaired mmdrop in mmu_interval_notifier_remove frees the active mm and its PGD while other threads of that process still run with CR3 aimed at it. Reclaiming the freed PGD gives a write primitive.\nA:H - __mmdrop of current->mm, reached from radeon_gem_object_free on the failed userptr ioctl, runs mm_free_pgd and free_mm on the live address space. The ioctl then keeps using that mm and returns to user mode, which oopses the kernel."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
