            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nnet/sched: sch_qfq: prevent aggregate self-replacement\n\nqfq_change_class() snapshots the current aggregate settings while\nholding the qdisc tree lock, but drops the lock before selecting the\ndestination aggregate. During that gap, qfq_enqueue() can move the\nclass to an aggregate matching the requested settings.\n\nWhen qfq_change_class() resumes, qfq_find_agg() then returns cl->agg.\nIf it is a singleton, qfq_deact_rm_from_agg() frees the aggregate\nbefore qfq_add_to_agg() immediately accesses the same pointer, causing\na use-after-free.\n\nWhile holding the tree lock, skip the replacement when the destination\nis already the current aggregate. Any estimator replacement has already\ncompleted, so the class change can finish normally."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - qfq_change_class(), the function the fix changes, is qfq_class_ops.change and is called from __tc_ctl_tclass() for RTM_NEWTCLASS. The other half of the race is a locally transmitted skb: __dev_xmit_skb() calls qfq_enqueue(), which calls qfq_change_agg() when qdisc_pkt_len() is greater than cl->agg->lmax.\nAC:L - The attacker creates one qfq class and sends RTM_NEWTCLASS with a larger TCA_QFQ_LMAX and the same weight. qfq_change_class() snapshots old_lmax under sch_tree_lock(), drops the lock, and the attacker's transmit runs qfq_change_agg() so the class becomes the sole member of an aggregate with that lmax and weight before qfq_find_agg() returns cl->agg. TCA_RATE also runs gen_replace_estimator() in the gap.\nPR:L - qfq_change_class() runs only from __tc_ctl_tclass() on RTM_NEWTCLASS, and rtnetlink_rcv_msg() accepts that message only when netlink_net_capable() sees CAP_NET_ADMIN in the network namespace's user namespace. Creating that namespace with unshare gives an unprivileged process the capability.\nUI:N - The same attacker sends RTM_NEWTCLASS and transmits the skb. qfq_classify() selects the class from skb->priority, which the sender sets to the class id.\nS:U - qfq_destroy_agg() kfree()s a qfq_aggregate of the caller's qdisc and qfq_add_to_agg() corrupts that same kernel heap. The impacted component is the kernel that handled the netlink message.\nC:H - When num_classes is 1, qfq_rm_from_agg() kfree()s the aggregate and qfq_add_to_agg() leaves the freed address in cl->agg. qfq_dump_class() copies agg->class_weight and agg->lmax to the netlink reply, and qfq_enqueue() reads agg->lmax, agg->active, and agg->grp, so reclaiming the slab slot discloses heap bytes and follows attacker-chosen pointers.\nI:H - qfq_update_agg() writes budgetmax, inv_w, and num_classes into the freed qfq_aggregate. On a later qfq_enqueue(), list_add_tail() stores cl->alist through agg->active and qfq_activate_agg() does hlist_add_head() of agg->next onto agg->grp->slots; after reclaim those are writes to attacker-chosen addresses.\nA:H - qfq_add_to_agg() and the next qfq_enqueue() or qfq_dequeue() dereference cl->agg, agg->active, and agg->grp after qfq_destroy_agg() has kfree()d that aggregate. The dangling list and group pointers oops the kernel, and the attacker can issue the class change again."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
