    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:38.683Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/filemap: finish fault cleanup before dropping the fault lock\n\nOn a retried fault, a fatal signal interrupting the folio lock wait\nmakes lock_folio_maybe_drop_mmap() drop the fault lock without pinning\nthe file. However, filemap_fault() may still need to release\nmapping->invalidate_lock. A concurrent munmap() can release the last\nfile reference and free the mapping before that unlock.\n\nDefer dropping the fault lock to filemap_fault()'s retry cleanup, after\nreleasing invalidate_lock. If the fault lock was already dropped for\nI/O, release the existing file reference instead. Keep the fault lock\nheld for FAULT_FLAG_RETRY_NOWAIT as before."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - The triggering access is a userspace load of a file mmap: do_user_addr_fault() retries with FAULT_FLAG_TRIED into handle_mm_fault(), do_read_fault(), __do_fault(), and filemap_fault() on that task's vm_file.\nAC:L - The same task forces the retry by faulting a file page that is not in cache, so the FAULT_FLAG_TRIED attempt blocks in __folio_lock_killable() inside lock_folio_maybe_drop_mmap() with mapping->invalidate_lock held, then a second thread munmaps after SIGKILL makes release_fault_lock() drop the mmap lock.\nPR:L - generic_file_mmap() sets generic_file_vm_ops.fault to filemap_fault with no capability check, and FAULT_FLAG_DEFAULT includes FAULT_FLAG_KILLABLE, so any local user who can read and mmap a file reaches the wait.\nUI:N - The fault, the SIGKILL that makes __folio_lock_killable() return -EINTR, and the munmap() that drops the last reference in remove_vma() are all actions of the attacking process on a file it already has open.\nS:U - The corrupted object is the faulting task's address_space in the host kernel, reached by up_read() of mapping->invalidate_lock during that task's own page fault.\nC:H - After remove_vma() fputs the last reference and evict() destroys the inode, filemap_invalidate_unlock_shared() calls up_read() on the freed mapping->invalidate_lock. rwsem_mark_wake() then reads first_waiter, waiter->task, and the waiter list from memory the attacker can reclaim.\nI:H - __up_read() subtracts RWSEM_READER_BIAS from the freed rwsem count and, when only RWSEM_FLAG_WAITERS remains, rwsem_mark_wake() calls list_move_tail() on waiter->list and updates sem->first_waiter, writing attacker-controlled kernel pointers.\nA:H - up_read() on mapping->invalidate_lock runs after the inode that embeds the address_space has been freed by the last fput() in remove_vma(), so the fault path dereferences freed or reused slab memory and oopses."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
                    "product": "Linux",
                    "vendor": "Linux",
                    "defaultStatus": "unaffected",
