        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.430Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:50:47.785Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:19.219Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:06:19.219Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/khugepaged: flush deferred unmaps before dropping a failed folio\n\ncollapse_file() can fail its reference-count or dirty-folio check after\nunmapping with TTU_BATCH_FLUSH.  Both paths put back the isolated folio,\nthen unlock it and drop the lookup reference before reaching the common\ntry_to_unmap_flush().\n\nThe page-cache reference does not keep the folio stable once the lock is\nreleased.  Another collapse can replace and free it.  With its PTEs\nalready gone, that collapse cannot flush the first task's per-task TLB\nbatch, and retract_page_tables() skips short or unaligned VMAs.  A CPU can\ntherefore retain a user translation to the freed folio.  This has been\nreproduced with unprivileged MADV_COLLAPSE on a memfd.\n\nFlush at out_unlock while the lookup reference and folio lock are still\nheld.  The common flush continues to cover the accumulated pagelist on\nboth success and rollback, preserving batching on successful collapses."
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7.8,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - madvise(MADV_COLLAPSE) reaches collapse_file() through do_madvise(), madvise_collapse(), and collapse_scan_file(). collapse_file() unmaps the caller's memfd folio via try_to_unmap(TTU_BATCH_FLUSH) and drops it on the post-unmap folio_ref_count() failure before try_to_unmap_flush().\nAC:L - The caller bumps the folio refcount during alloc_charge_folio() so collapse_file() fails the post-unmap folio_ref_count() check, drops that pin, and a second MADV_COLLAPSE frees the folio via folio_put_refs() while the first task's tlb_ubc is still pending. Running the memfd on another CPU makes arch_tlbbatch_should_defer() true, and a short or unaligned VMA makes retract_page_tables() skip pmdp_collapse_flush().\nPR:L - SYSCALL_DEFINE3(madvise) and memfd_create() perform no capable() check. MADV_COLLAPSE passes TVA_FORCED_COLLAPSE, so vma_thp_disabled() allows it and shmem_huge_force makes shmem_allowable_huge_orders() return a PMD order for an unprivileged memfd.\nUI:N - The attacker calls memfd_create(), mmap(), and madvise(MADV_COLLAPSE) on a mapping it owns, and that same caller is the task that enters collapse_file().\nS:U - The stale TLB entry is in the caller's mm, and the folio released by folio_put_refs() returns to that kernel's buddy allocator. The dangling translation and the reused page are both inside the same kernel security authority.\nC:H - try_to_unmap() clears the memfd PTEs and, under TTU_BATCH_FLUSH, only queues them in current->tlb_ubc via set_tlb_ubc_flush_pending(). Once the later collapse's folio_put_refs() frees the folio, a CPU that still caches the translation reads the page after the buddy allocator reuses it.\nI:H - Those cached entries stay writable: try_to_unmap() leaves remote CPUs able to write the folio until try_to_unmap_flush(), and a MAP_SHARED PROT_WRITE memfd mapping installs writable PTEs. A store hits the physical page after folio_put_refs() has freed it.\nA:H - folio_put_refs() in the successful collapse frees the folio while a user TLB entry still names it. A later store or load through that entry corrupts a reused slab object or page-table page and oopses the kernel."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
