{
    "dataType": "CVE_RECORD",
    "dataVersion": "5.2",
    "cveMetadata": {
        "cveId": "CVE-2026-99659",
        "assignerOrgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
        "state": "PUBLISHED",
        "assignerShortName": "Linux",
        "dateReserved": "2026-09-25T10:25:14.427Z",
        "datePublished": "2026-11-20T05:50:10.874Z",
        "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:05:40.418Z"
    },
    "containers": {
        "cna": {
            "providerMetadata": {
                "orgId": "416baaa9-dc9f-4396-8d5f-8c081fb06d67",
                "shortName": "Linux",
                "dateUpdated": "2026-11-23T08:05:40.418Z"
            },
            "descriptions": [
                {
                    "lang": "en",
                    "value": "In the Linux kernel, the following vulnerability has been resolved:\n\nmm/mmu_gather: fix flushing ranges with 4 GiB or larger\n\n__tlb_adjust_range() takes the size of the range which has to be flushed\nas an unsigned int. Its callers, tlb_flush_{pte,pmd,pud,p4d}_range(), get\nand pass an unsigned long. Consequently, a range that is 4 GiB or larger\ngets truncated, possibly to 0.\n\nOn arm64 with hugetlb, a 64k kernel can have 16 GiB hugetlb folios\nmapped contiguously at the PMD level and flush them when unmapping, and a\n16k kernel can flush 64 GiB PUDs through tlb_unshare_pmd_ptdesc(). So\nat least these two scenarios are affected.\n\nFix it by flushing the proper range instead of the truncated one by\nproperly using \"unsigned long\" for range_size.\n\nFix generated by an LLM and sent by Josef Bacik in private.\n\n[ david: use two-tab indent, simplify description ]"
                }
            ],
            "metrics": [
                {
                    "cvssV3_1": {
                        "version": "3.1",
                        "vectorString": "CVSS:3.1/AV:L/AC:H/PR:L/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:H/A:H",
                        "baseScore": 7,
                        "baseSeverity": "HIGH"
                    },
                    "scenarios": [
                        {
                            "lang": "en",
                            "value": "AV:L - munmap() of a hugetlb VMA reaches the bug through do_vmi_munmap(), unmap_region(), unmap_vmas(), __unmap_hugepage_range(), hugetlb_unmap_range(), and tlb_remove_huge_tlb_entry(), which passes huge_page_size() into __tlb_adjust_range(). No remote protocol supplies that size.\nAC:H - The unsigned int truncation in __tlb_adjust_range() fires only for a single range of at least 4GiB: arm64 64K CONT_PMD 16GiB folios, or PUD_SIZE (64GiB) from tlb_unshare_pmd_ptdesc() on 16K. Those folios exist only if root filled nr_hugepages or hugetlb_cma; the attacker cannot create them.\nPR:L - MAP_HUGETLB in ksys_mmap_pgoff() uses hugetlb_file_setup() with HUGETLB_ANONHUGE_INODE and never calls capable(). A local user who can already allocate from the pool can munmap it; CAP_IPC_LOCK is required only for SHM_HUGETLB, which this path does not use.\nUI:N - The attacker faults in their own mapping and calls munmap(). free_huge_folio() runs from tlb_flush_mmu() with no other user action; the stale TLB belongs to the attacking mm.\nS:U - The missed invalidation leaves a stage-1 translation in the unmapping mm's ASID after hugetlb_unmap_range() clears the PTEs and arm64 tlb_flush() skips the range. The impact stays in that host address space.\nC:H - huge_ptep_get_and_clear() removes the PTEs, then tlb_flush_mmu() frees the folio via free_huge_folio() after arm64 tlb_flush() computes pages=(end-start)>>PAGE_SHIFT as 0. The old VA still reads the physical page after the pool reallocates it.\nI:H - The truncated range leaves a writable TLB entry. After free_huge_folio() enqueues the folio, stores through the old VA modify the same physical memory once another hugetlb allocation receives it.\nA:H - tlb_finish_mmu() calls free_huge_folio() after arm64 tlb_flush() skips the range: __flush_tlb_range_op() runs only while addr!=end, and the truncated size makes them equal. That use-after-free of the folio can crash whoever next receives the page."
                        }
                    ]
                }
            ],
            "affected": [
                {
